{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01TpNXTEZNKKhgmM3vqaCwaz", "type": "tool_result", "content": "Web search results for query: \"TinyStories\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co:443/papers/2305.07759\"},{\"title\":\"Computer Science > Computation and Language\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.07759\"},{\"title\":\"Link to original article\",\"url\":\"https://www.podbean.[scrubbed:long-base64]\"},{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2305.07759\"},{\"title\":\"huggingface.co\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/roneneldan/TinyStories\"},{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://forum.devtalk.com/t/tinystories-how-small-can-language-models-be-and-still-speak-coherent-english/109566\"},{\"title\":\"Tiny Book of Tiny Stories: Volume 3\",\"url\":\"https://www.hitrecord.org/store/tinystories\"},{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://leonericsson.github.io/blog/2023-08-07-tinystories\"},{\"title\":\"lemmy.world\",\"url\":\"https://lemmy.world/post/370834\"},{\"title\":\"tiny stories\",\"url\":\"https://lindac.substack.com/t/tiny-stories\"},{\"title\":\"tinystories 1m\",\"url\":\"https://www.promptlayer.com/models/tinystories-1m\"},{\"title\":\"Introducing SimpleStories\",\"url\":\"https://tdooms.github.io/research/simplestories\"},{\"title\":\"r/MachineLearning May 16 '23\",\"url\":\"https://lr.us.psf.lt/r/MachineLearning/comments/13j0spj/r_tiny_language_models_below_10m_parameters_or\"},{\"title\":\"05 tinystories\",\"url\":\"https://desty.github.io/study-tiny-llm/en/part2/05-tinystories/\"},{\"title\":\"meilu.jpshuntong.com\",\"url\":\"https://meilu.jpshuntong.com/url-68747470733a2f2f61727869762e6f7267/pdf/2305.07759.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"a tiny of tiny stories\",\"url\":\"https://internal-mongodb.champds.com/archive-ga-23-03/a-tiny-of-tiny-stories.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"docs.google.com\",\"url\":\"https://docs.google.[scrubbed:long-base64]?format=pdf\"}]\n\nTinyStories is a synthetic dataset of short children's stories, introduced in a 2023 Microsoft Research paper by Ronen Eldan and Yuanzhi Li. The results cover the paper, the dataset, and the models trained on it.\n\n- **The dataset:** It was generated by GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 and uses vocabulary that most 3- to 4-year-olds would understand. The dataset is available on Huggingface named TinyStories.\n- **The research question:** The paper asks whether coherent English requires very large models. Models around 125M parameters often can rarely generate coherent and consistent English text beyond a few words even after extensive training.\n- **Findings:** Small models trained on the data can produce fluent text. One summary notes that the largest model trained on TinyStories (with 80M parameters) reaches almost perfect score on grammar and consistency but falls well behind GPT-4 on creativity.\n- **Evaluation method:** The authors use GPT-4 to grade model output the way a teacher would grade student stories, scoring dimensions such as grammar, creativity, and consistency. They suggest a framework which uses GPT-4 to grade the content generated by these models as if those were stories written by students and graded by a (human) teacher.\n- **Released models:** Several small models are on Hugging Face, including TinyStories-1M through 33M parameter versions and TinyStories-Instruct variants.\n- **Influence:** Later work treats it as an early example of synthetic data. One course notes that after TinyStories, synthetic data became a standard tool. SimpleStories, a successor project, describes TinyStories as a dataset of children's stories that tiny language models (~10 million parameter) trained on this dataset can generate coherent and creative stories.\n- **Caveats:** Some users have reported that the models stop after only a few words, and one project notes that the original contains some grammatical errors.\n\nThe paper is on arXiv (2305.07759), and the dataset and models are on Hugging Face under roneneldan. Note that \"tiny stories\" also appears as an unrelated writing game and a hashtag for very short fiction, so results with those terms are different projects.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "dcd1114f-1dea-448d-8ab8-2c911ec1db65", "uuid": "4287cc04-fcdc-49d1-9dbb-2eaec178eac2", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T13:39:35.715Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "TinyStories", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01XoH6Eoqn2wZM3n2AZbM1JH", "content": [{"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://huggingface.co:443/papers/2305.07759"}, {"title": "Computer Science > Computation and Language", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.07759"}, {"title": "Link to original article", "url": "https://www.podbean.[scrubbed:long-base64]"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2305.07759"}, {"title": "huggingface.co", "url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/roneneldan/TinyStories"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://forum.devtalk.com/t/tinystories-how-small-can-language-models-be-and-still-speak-coherent-english/109566"}, {"title": "Tiny Book of Tiny Stories: Volume 3", "url": "https://www.hitrecord.org/store/tinystories"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://leonericsson.github.io/blog/2023-08-07-tinystories"}, {"title": "lemmy.world", "url": "https://lemmy.world/post/370834"}, {"title": "tiny stories", "url": "https://lindac.substack.com/t/tiny-stories"}, {"title": "tinystories 1m", "url": "https://www.promptlayer.com/models/tinystories-1m"}, {"title": "Introducing SimpleStories", "url": "https://tdooms.github.io/research/simplestories"}, {"title": "r/MachineLearning May 16 '23", "url": "https://lr.us.psf.lt/r/MachineLearning/comments/13j0spj/r_tiny_language_models_below_10m_parameters_or"}, {"title": "05 tinystories", "url": "https://desty.github.io/study-tiny-llm/en/part2/05-tinystories/"}, {"title": "meilu.jpshuntong.com", "url": "https://meilu.jpshuntong.com/url-68747470733a2f2f61727869762e6f7267/pdf/2305.07759.pdf"}, {"title": "a tiny of tiny stories", "url": "https://internal-mongodb.champds.com/archive-ga-23-03/a-tiny-of-tiny-stories.pdf"}, {"title": "docs.google.com", "url": "https://docs.google.[scrubbed:long-base64]?format=pdf"}]}, "TinyStories is a synthetic dataset of short children's stories, introduced in a 2023 Microsoft Research paper by Ronen Eldan and Yuanzhi Li. The results cover the paper, the dataset, and the models trained on it.\n\n- **The dataset:** It was generated by GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 and uses vocabulary that most 3- to 4-year-olds would understand. The dataset is available on Huggingface named TinyStories.\n- **The research question:** The paper asks whether coherent English requires very large models. Models around 125M parameters often can rarely generate coherent and consistent English text beyond a few words even after extensive training.\n- **Findings:** Small models trained on the data can produce fluent text. One summary notes that the largest model trained on TinyStories (with 80M parameters) reaches almost perfect score on grammar and consistency but falls well behind GPT-4 on creativity.\n- **Evaluation method:** The authors use GPT-4 to grade model output the way a teacher would grade student stories, scoring dimensions such as grammar, creativity, and consistency. They suggest a framework which uses GPT-4 to grade the content generated by these models as if those were stories written by students and graded by a (human) teacher.\n- **Released models:** Several small models are on Hugging Face, including TinyStories-1M through 33M parameter versions and TinyStories-Instruct variants.\n- **Influence:** Later work treats it as an early example of synthetic data. One course notes that after TinyStories, synthetic data became a standard tool. SimpleStories, a successor project, describes TinyStories as a dataset of children's stories that tiny language models (~10 million parameter) trained on this dataset can generate coherent and creative stories.\n- **Caveats:** Some users have reported that the models stop after only a few words, and one project notes that the original contains some grammatical errors.\n\nThe paper is on arXiv (2305.07759), and the dataset and models are on Hugging Face under roneneldan. Note that \"tiny stories\" also appears as an unrelated writing game and a hashtag for very short fiction, so results with those terms are different projects."], "durationSeconds": 13.028000612999996, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01TpNXTEZNKKhgmM3vqaCwaz", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "config", "reason_type": "rule"}}]}
